From 437de3151e2943b94d80681a415e4c762cec31a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 11:11:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 209/210] media: amlogic: amvdec: add S4 support

The S4 decoder is register compatible with SM1, with these differences:

- The VDEC_1 and VDEC_HEVC power domains are switched by the secure
  monitor, which also handles isolation, instead of the AO RTI sleep
  and isolation bits. Power the VDEC memories before the core reset,
  hold the reset for 20-100us, and only set the low ten bits of
  DOS_GCLK_EN0. Enable the HEVC core clock before powering its domain,
  and after the HEVC sub-engine reset pulse the DMC-side HEVC pipeline
  resets from the reset controller in place of the G12B RESET7 level
  bits.
- The DMC request control window is described in DT, and its channel
  status register moved to 0x338. Map the G12 DMC at probe as well, so
  the park helpers no longer keep their own mappings.
- There is no ESPARSER. Copy each request into the VIFIFO ring and
  publish the write pointer to the core, with the stream control bits
  set for a host-fed stream. On VDEC_1, keep the VIFIFO in the manual
  write pointer mode that stbuf_power_up() selects and publish the
  pointer through VLD_MEM_VIFIFO_WP. Hold the HEVC core until the first
  input is in the FIFO: started earlier, the firmware can find the FIFO
  empty, report it, and wait for a host that has nothing to resume it
  with.
- Microcode ships as a signed package that only the secure world can
  decrypt, and plaintext images loaded by IMEM DMA do not run reliably.
  Add a per-format secure-world microcode index, and when it is set ask
  the secure monitor (fast call 0xb200000f) to load the image into the
  AMRISC instead. A userspace loader must first pass the package to the
  Amlogic video firmware trusted application; without that the call
  returns TEE_ERROR_BAD_STATE and the session fails to start. The secure
  world owns the microcode swap area, so do not program AV_SCRATCH_G on
  this path. Plaintext S4 images carry a 512-byte header with a "CODE"
  magic and the payload size; skip it when present.

The package has H.264 multi microcode that parses in-band SPS/PPS like
the SM1 build, multi-instance MPEG-1/2 microcode tagged 'M12M' for
VDEC_1, and only MMU builds of HEVC and VP9 for the HEVC core. HEVC is
tried first as the build with swapped-in sections ('265S', loaded with
the swap flag), then as the plain MMU build. These differ from the
images the driver was written for:

- The MMU builds always expect compressed references in the frame MMU.
  Use compressed references plus an NV12 double write for 8-bit as well
  as 10-bit content when the microcode comes from the secure world.
- The HEVC parameter block has room for 12 tile columns instead of 8.
  Drop the extra entries so the rest of the block keeps its layout.
- VP9 treats a zero HEVC_DECODE_SIZE as a limit rather than as
  unlimited, and stops after the first frame header. Start without a
  limit; the driver lowers it for each picture as before.

Two HEVC paths also assumed the G12A-family cores. The workspace setup
is replayed once the frame MMU map is allocated to program
HEVC_ASSIST_MMU_MAP_ADDR, but only on G12A, G12B and SM1, so on S4 the
MMU read its page list from physical address 0. Replay it on every SoC
from G12A on. The S4 HEVC core can also route the double write through
a second frame MMU, selected by HEVC_SAO_CTRL5 bit 15 and HEVC_SAO_CTRL9
bit 10, whose page map the driver never programs. Clear both bits so
the double write always goes to the capture buffer.

Enable H.264, MPEG-2, HEVC and VP9 on S4, with NV12 capture and AM21C
capture for HEVC and VP9.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
